n\nA=8:\n\nk-10 \u22649/8\u22481.125 => k-10 \u22641 =>k\u226411. So same as above. k=10,11. For k=11, C=8*1=8. 80 becomes 880=11*80. k=11.\n\nA=9:\n\nk-10 \u22649/9=1 =>k-10 \u22641 =>k\u226411. So k=10,11. For k=11, C=9*1=9. 90 becomes 990=11*90. k=11.\n\nSo, in case B=0, the maximum k is 19 when A=1, B=0, C=9. Let's check this: original number is 10, inserting 9 in the middle makes 190. 190 divided by 10 is 19. So yes, k=19 here.\n\nBut the problem says \"for some two-digit numbers\", so k=19 is possible here. But we need to check other cases where B\u22600 because maybe there's a higher k possible. Wait, is 19 the maximum? Let's check other cases.\n\nCase 2: B\u22600. So, we need to have B*(k -1) divisible by 10. Since B\u22600, then (k -1) must compensate for the factors. Let's recall that 10=2*5. So, for B*(k -1) to be divisible by 10, B must supply at least one factor (either 2 or 5) and (k -1) must supply the other. For example:\n\nIf B is even (contains a factor of 2), then (k -1) must be a multiple of 5.\n\nIf B is a multiple of 5 (i.e., B=5), then (k -1) must be even (i.e., multiple of 2).\n\nIf B is a multiple of both 2 and 5 (i.e., B=0), which is covered in case 1.\n\nSince B is from 1 to 9 here (excluding 0), so possible B values:\n\n- If B is even (2,4,6,8), then (k -1) must be a multiple of 5.\n\n- If B is 5, then (k -1) must be even.\n\n- If B is odd and not 5 (1,3,7,9), then since B contributes neither 2 nor 5, (k -1) must supply both 2 and 5, i.e., (k -1) must be a multiple of 10. But since B is non-zero and not divisible by 2 or 5, the only way B*(k -1) is divisible by 10 is if (k -1) is divisible by 10. However, in this case, since B is 1,3,7,9, which are coprime with 10, (k -1) must be divisible by 10. So, (k -1) \u22610 mod 10 => k=11,21,... but since k is multiplied by the original two-digit number (which is at least 10), and the resulting three-digit number is at most 999 (since inserting a digit into a two-digit number can give up to 999, e.g., 99 becomes 909). Wait, but the original two-digit number can be up to 99, and inserting a digit gives up to 999. So, k can be up to 999 / 10 = 99.9, so k maximum possible is 99, but likely much lower.\n\nBut let's see.\n\nSo, for B even (2,4,6,8): (k -1) multiple of 5 => k=5m +1, where m is integer.\n\nFor B=5: (k -1) even => k=2m +1, so k is odd.\n\nFor B odd (1,3,7,9): (k -1) multiple of 10 => k=10m +1.\n\nWe can analyze these cases.\n\nLet me start with B even (2,4,6,8). So, k=5m +1.\n\nFrom the original equation:\n\n100A +10C + B = k*(10A + B)\n\nWe can write:\n\n100A +10C + B = (5m +1)(10A + B)\n\nExpanding the right-hand side:\n\n(5m +1)(10A + B) = 5m*(10A + B) + 10A + B\n\nSo,\n\n100A +10C + B = 50mA +5mB +10A + B\n\nSubtracting the right-hand side from both sides:\n\n100A +10C + B -50mA -5mB -10A -B =0\n\nSimplify:\n\n(100A -50mA -10A) + (10C) + (B -5mB -B) =0\n\nSo,\n\nA*(100 -50m -10) +10C -5mB =0\n\nWhich simplifies to:\n\nA*(90 -50m) +10C -5mB =0\n\nDivide all terms by 5:\n\nA*(18 -10m) + 2C - mB =0\n\nSo,\n\nA*(18 -10m) +2C = mB\n\nLet me rearrange:\n\n2C = mB - A*(18 -10m)\n\nHmm, this seems a bit complex. Maybe let's plug in possible m values. Since k=5m +1, and k must be a positive integer. Also, the three-digit number 100A +10C + B must be larger than the original two-digit number 10A + B, so k must be at least 2? Wait, but if inserting a digit in the middle, depending on C, it could be as low as 100A + 0 + B, which is 100A + B. So, if A=1, B=0, we had k=19. But for B\u22600, maybe k can be smaller or larger. Wait, let's check.\n\nWait, if we take A=1, B=5, and insert C= something, perhaps k could be higher. Let's see.\n\nBut maybe instead of getting lost in algebra, let's try specific values.\n\nLet me first handle the case where B is even (2,4,6,8) and k=5m +1. Let's look for possible high k values.\n\nSuppose m is as large as possible. Let's see:\n\nk=5m +1. To maximize k, we need to maximize m. But m is constrained by the equation. Let's se