ided by 15 is indeed 13. So k=13 here. So this is a valid case. So 13 is higher than the previous 11, but still less than 19.\n\nNow, check if we can get higher k with other A and B=5.\n\nLet's try A=2, B=5. Original number 25. Insert C to get 2C5. So 200 +10C +5=205 +10C. This must equal k*25.\n\nSo, 205 +10C =25k => k=(205 +10C)/25. Let's check for which C this is integer.\n\nSimplify: 205 +10C =25k => 41 +2C =5k. So 5k=41 +2C. So 41 +2C must be divisible by5. 41 mod5=1, so 1 +2C \u22610 mod5 =>2C\u22614 mod5 =>C\u22612 mod5. Therefore, C=2 or7.\n\nCheck C=2:41 +4=45. 45/5=9. So k=9. 2C5=225. 225=9*25. Correct.\n\nC=7:41 +14=55. 55/5=11. So k=11. 275=11*25. Correct.\n\nSo maximum k here is 11, which is lower than 13.\n\nNext, A=3, B=5. Original number 35. Insert C gives 3C5=300 +10C +5=305 +10C. So 305 +10C=35k.\n\nThus, 35k=305 +10C =>7k=61 +2C. So 61 +2C must be divisible by7. Let's compute 61 mod7=5, so 5 +2C \u22610 mod7 =>2C\u22612 mod7 =>C\u22611 mod7/ gcd(2,7)=1. So C\u22611 mod7. So C=1,8.\n\nC=1:61 +2=63. 63/7=9. k=9. 315=9*35. Correct.\n\nC=8:61 +16=77. 77/7=11. k=11. 385=11*35. Correct.\n\nSo k=11 here.\n\nSimilarly, A=4, B=5:45. Insert C:4C5=400 +10C +5=405 +10C. So 405 +10C=45k =>9k=81 +2C.\n\nThus, 81 +2C must be divisible by9. 81 is already divisible by9, so 2C must be divisible by9. So 2C\u22610 mod9 =>C\u22610 mod9/ gcd(2,9)=9. So C=0 or9.\n\nC=0:81 +0=81. 81/9=9. k=9. 405=9*45. Correct.\n\nC=9:81 +18=99. 99/9=11. k=11. 495=11*45. Correct.\n\nSo again, k=11.\n\nA=5, B=5:55. Insert C:5C5=500 +10C +5=505 +10C. So 505 +10C=55k.\n\nDivide both sides by5:101 +2C=11k. So 101 +2C must be divisible by11. 101 mod11=101 -9*11=101 -99=2. So 2 +2C\u22610 mod11 =>2C\u2261-2 mod11 =>2C\u22619 mod11 =>C\u2261(9/2) mod11. Since 2*6=12\u22611 mod11, inverse of2 is6. So C\u22619*6=54\u226110 mod11. But C is a digit, so C=10 is invalid. Therefore, no solution here.\n\nNo solution for A=5, B=5.\n\nA=6, B=5:65. Insert C:6C5=600 +10C +5=605 +10C. So 605 +10C=65k.\n\nDi