 - i if i \u2264 n/2, and \u03c3(i) = n/2 - i + (n + 1) otherwise. Then:\n\nFor i \u2264 n/2:\n\n\u03c4(i) = n/2 + i - (n/2 - i) = 2i\n\nFor i > n/2:\n\n\u03c3(i) = n/2 - i + n + 1 = (3n/2 +1) - i\n\n\u03c4(i) = n/2 + i - [(3n/2 +1) - i] = n/2 + i - 3n/2 -1 + i = -n -1 + 2i\n\nBut since i > n/2, let i = n/2 + k where k \u22651. Then:\n\n\u03c4(i) = -n -1 + 2(n/2 + k) = -n -1 + n + 2k = -1 + 2k\n\nBut k ranges from 1 to n/2, so \u03c4(i) ranges from 1 to n -1 (since k=1 gives \u03c4=1, k=n/2 gives \u03c4= n -1). However, when i = n/2 + k, \u03c4(i) = 2k -1, which for k=1 to n/2 gives odd numbers from 1 to n-1 (if n is even). But in our case, n=1960, which is even, so n/2=980. Thus, \u03c4(i) for i > n/2 would be the odd numbers from 1 to 1959. While \u03c4(i) for i \u2264 n/2 are the even numbers from 0 to 1960. Wait, n=1960, so i ranges from 0 to 1960. For i \u2264980, \u03c4(i)=2i which gives 0,2,...,1960. For i >980 (i=981 to 1960), \u03c4(i)=2k-1 where k=1 to 980, giving 1,3,...,1959. So combined, \u03c4 covers all numbers from 0 to 1960, each exactly once. Therefore, \u03c4 is a permutation.\n\nTherefore, this construction works. Therefore, \u03c3(i) = n/2 - i mod (n +1) defines a permutation \u03c3, and \u03c4(i) = n/2 + i - \u03c3(i) defines a permutation \u03c4. Therefore, the permutations exist.\n\nApplying this to our problem, since n=1960 (the sequences have 1961 numbers from a to a+1960 and b to b+1960), we can define the permutations as follows:\n\nFor the first sequence S = {a, a+1, ..., a+1960}, define the permutation \u03c3 such that S'_i = a + \u03c3(i), where \u03c3(i) = 980 - i mod 1961.\n\nSimilarly, for the second sequence T = {b, b+1, ..., b+1960}, the permutation \u03c4 is defined such that T'_i = b + \u03c4(i), where \u03c4(i) = 980 + i - \u03c3(i).\n\nAs shown earlier, \u03c4(i) will cover all numbers from 0 to 1960 exactly once, hence T'_i will cover all elements of T exactly once. Therefore, the sums S'_i + T'_i = a + \u03c3(i) + b + \u03c4