nsferred more efficiently than obtaining the ceiling of \\( n \\) divided by every current square distance (as each stone needs to ensure it reaches or passes \\( k \\) goals).\n\n5. **Summation of Moves**:\n   - To account for every stone's move, we need at least \\( \\lceil \\frac{n}{1} \\rceil \\), \\( \\lceil \\frac{n}{2} \\rceil \\), ..., \\( \\lceil \\frac{n}{n} \\rceil \\) operations.\n   - This yields the total number of minimal operations to ensure every stone reaches its goal destination within the constraints.\n\n6. **Conclusion**:\n   - The steps detailed thus guarantee that fewer operations would be insufficient to ensure every stone arrives at destination \\( n \\). Thus,\n\n\\[\n\\text{The minimum number of operations} \\geq \\left\\lceil \\frac{n}{1} \\right\\rceil + \\left\\lceil \\frac{n}{2} \\right\\rceil + \\cdots + \\left\\lceil \\frac{n}{n} \\right\\rceil\n\\]\n\nHence, we conclude the desired least sum/index to demonstrate \\( \\boxed{\\left\\lceil \\frac{n}{1} \\right\\rceil + \\left\\lceil \\frac{n}{2} \\right\\rceil + \\cdots + \\left\\lceil \\frac{n}{n} \\right\\rceil} \\).", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Each cell of a \\(2 \\times 5\\) grid of unit squares is to be colored white or black. Compute the number of such colorings for which no \\(2 \\times 2\\) square is a single color.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Alright, let me try to figure out how to solve this problem. So, we have a 2x5 grid, and each cell can be colored either white or black. We need to count the number of colorings where there's no 2x2 square that's all the same color. Hmm, okay.\n\nFirst, I know that without any restrictions, the total number of colorings would be 2^(2*5) = 2^10 = 1024. But now we have to exclude those colorings that have at least one 2x2 square which is all white or all black. But wait, the grid is 2x5, so how many 2x2 squares are there? Let me think. Since the grid is 2 rows high, vertically, the 2x2 squares can only shift horizontally. Each 2x2 square occupies 2 columns. In a 2x5 grid, the number of 2x2 squares would be 5 - 1 = 4? Wait, columns go from 1 to 5, so starting at column 1: columns 1-2, 2-3, 3-4, 4-5. Yeah, so there are 4 such 2x2 squares.\n\nSo each of these 4 positions could potentially be a monochromatic square. But we need to count all colorings where none of these 4 positions is monochromatic. This sounds like an inclusion-exclusion problem. Because we need to subtract the colorings that have at least one monochromatic 2x2 square, then add back those that have two overlapping ones, and so on.\n\nLet me recall the inclusion-exclusion principle. For any collection of sets, the size of their union is the sum of the sizes of each set minus the sum of the sizes of all pairwise intersections plus the sum of all triple intersections, and so on, alternating signs until the intersection of all sets is considered.\n\nSo in this case, let's define A_i as the set of colorings where the i-th 2x2 square is monochromatic. Then the number we want is total colorings minus |A1 \u222a A2 \u222a A3 \u222a A4|. By inclusion-exclusion:\n\n|A1 \u222a A2 \u222a A3 \u222a A4| = \u03a3|Ai| - \u03a3|Ai \u2229 Aj| + \u03a3|Ai \u2229 Aj \u2229 Ak| - ... + (-1)^{n+1}|A1 \u2229 A2 \u2229 A3 \u2229 A4}|.\n\nTherefore, the desired count is 2^10 - [\u03a3|Ai| - \u03a3|Ai \u2229 Aj| + \u03a3|Ai \u2229 Aj \u2229 Ak| - |A1 \u2229 A2 \u2229 A3 \u2229 A4}|].\n\nSo first, let's compute |Ai| for each i. Each Ai is the set of colorings where the i-th 2x2 square is all white or all black. For a single 2x2 square, there are 2 monochromatic colorings. Once that 2x2 square is fixed, the rest of the grid can be colored freely. But in the 2x5 grid, the i-th 2x2 square is columns i and i+1. So if we fix the color of those 4 cells (2 rows, 2 columns), the remaining cells are the other 3 columns (since the grid has 5 columns). Wait, no: the grid is 2 rows and 5 columns. If we fix