n\nNow, among these, which pairs overlap? Let's see:\n\n- (1,2): the 2x2 squares are columns 1-2 and 2-3. They overlap in column 2.\n\n- (1,3): columns 1-2 and 3-4. No overlap.\n\n- (1,4): columns 1-2 and 4-5. No overlap.\n\n- (2,3): columns 2-3 and 3-4. Overlap in column 3.\n\n- (2,4): columns 2-3 and 4-5. No overlap.\n\n- (3,4): columns 3-4 and 4-5. Overlap in column 4.\n\nSo overlapping pairs are (1,2), (2,3), (3,4). The others are non-overlapping.\n\nSo for the intersections, overlapping pairs will have overlapping 2x2 squares, meaning that the combined region they cover is more than 2 columns. Let me see:\n\nFor example, (1,2): covers columns 1-3 (since 1-2 and 2-3). So columns 1,2,3. Each of these pairs of columns has a 2x2 square. Similarly, (2,3) covers columns 2-4, and (3,4) covers 3-5.\n\nTherefore, when two 2x2 squares are adjacent, their union covers 3 columns, and their intersection is a 2x1 column.\n\nIn such cases, if both 2x2 squares are monochromatic, what constraints does that impose?\n\nSuppose both the first 2x2 (columns 1-2) and the second 2x2 (columns 2-3) are monochromatic. Let's denote the color of the first 2x2 as C1 (either all white or all black), and the color of the second 2x2 as C2. However, column 2 is shared between both 2x2 squares. So the middle column (column 2) must be the same color in both C1 and C2. Therefore, if the first 2x2 is C1, then column 2 (both cells) must be C1. The second 2x2 is columns 2-3, so column 2 is C1, and column 3 must be C2. But column 2 is part of the second 2x2 square, so if the second 2x2 is monochromatic, column 2 and column 3 must both be C2. But column 2 is already C1. Therefore, unless C1 = C2, this is impossible. So if C1 = C2, then columns 1-2-3 must all be color C1. Therefore, the three columns 1,2,3 must all be color C1. Therefore, the combined 2x3 grid (columns 1-3) is all color C1. Then, the remaining columns 4 and 5 can be colored freely.\n\nWait, but hold on. Let me think again. If both the first and second 2x2 squares are monochromatic, then the first 2x2 (columns 1-2) must be all C1, and the second 2x2 (columns 2-3) must be all C2. But columns 2 is shared. So the cells in column 2 (both rows) must be both C1 and C2. Therefore, unless C1 = C2, this is impossible. So the only way for both 2x2 squares to be monochromatic is if C1 = C2, and columns 1,2,3 are all C1. Therefore, columns 1-3 form a 2x3 rectangle of color C1, and columns 4-5 are free. Each of these columns (4 and 5) has 2 cells, so 2^4 possibilities. So the number of colorings where both A1 and A2 occur is 2 (choices for C1) * 2^4 = 32.\n\nWait, but columns 4 and 5: each has 2 cells, so 4 cells total, 2^4=16. So 2 * 16 = 32. So |A1 \u2229 A2| = 32.\n\nSimilarly, for overlapping pairs (2,3) and (3,4), the same logic applies. For example, |A2 \u2229 A3| would require columns 2-4 to be all one color, and columns 5 is free? Wait, columns 2-4: if A2 is columns 2-3, and A3 is columns 3-4, then their union is columns 2-4. So similar to before, columns 2-4 must be all the same color, and columns 1 and 5 are free. So columns 1 has 2 cells, column 5 has 2 cells, so 4 cells total, 2^4=16. So |A2 \u2229 A3| = 2 * 16 = 32. Similarly, |A3 \u2229 A4| is columns 3-5 being all one color, and columns 1-2 are free. Wait, columns 3-5: that's 3 columns. Wait, A3 is columns 3-4, A4 is columns 4-5. So overlapping at column 4. So similar to before, columns 3-4-5 must all be the same color. So columns 3-5 are all color C, and columns 1-2 are free. Columns 1-2: 4 cells, 2^4=16. So |A3 \u2229 A4|= 2 * 16 = 32.\n\nSo for overlapping pairs, each intersection has 32 colorings.\n\nNow, what about non-overlapping pairs? For example, (1,3). The 2x2 squares are columns 1-2 and 3-4. These are separated by column 2. So they don't overlap. Therefore, the colorings where both 2x2 squares are monochromatic. In this case, the two 2x2 squares can be colored independently. However, we have to check if they share any cells. Since columns 1-2 and 3-4 are separate, the only overlapping column would be if there's a column in between. But in this case, no. So columns 1-2 and 3-4 are separate. Therefore, the constraints are independent. So the number of colorings would be 2 (for first 2x2) * 2 (for second 2x2) * 2^(remaining cells). Let's see.\n\nIf we fix columns 1-2 as monochromatic (color C1) and columns 3-4 as monochromatic (color C2), then the remaining columns are 5. Wait, no: the grid is 2x5. If we fix columns 1-2 and 3-4, the remaining column is column 5. So column 5 has 2 cells, which can be colored freely: 2^2=4.\n\nBut also, between columns 1-2 and 3-4, there's column 2 and column 3? Wait, no. Columns 1-2, fixed as C1; columns 3-4 fixed as C2. The remaining column is column 5. Wait, no: columns 1-2 (fixed), 3-4 (fixed), and column 5 (free). So columns fixed: 1,2,3,4. Wait, no: columns 1-2 and 3-4. So columns 1,2,3,4 are fixed? Wait, if columns 1-2 are fixed as C1, and columns 3-4 as C2, then columns 1,2,3,4 are all fixed. Column 5 is free. Each of the fixed columns: columns 1-2 (both rows) are color C1; columns 3-4 (both rows) are color C2. Therefore, column 5 has 2 cells, free to choose. So the total number of colorings is 2 (C1) * 2 (C2) * 2^2 (column 5) = 2*2*4=16. So |A1 \u2229 A3| = 16.\n\nWait, but hold on. Let me check again. If columns 1-2 are fixed to a single color C1, that's 2 possibilities (all white or all black). Similarly, columns 3-4 are fixed to C2, another 2 possibilities. Then column 5 is free, 2 cells, 2^2=4. So total 2*2*4=16. Yes.\n\nSimilarly, for other non-overlapping pairs. Let's take (1,4): columns 1-2 and 4-5. These are separated by column 3. So fixing columns 1-2 as C1, columns 4-5 as C2, and column 3 is free. Column 3 has 2 cells, so 2^2=4. Therefore, |A1 \u2229 A4| = 2*2*4=16. Similarly, (2,4): columns 2-3 and 4-5. Fixing columns 2-3 as C1, columns 4-5 as C2. The remaining columns are 1 and 5? Wait, no: columns 2-3 and 4-5. Wait, the grid is columns 1 to 5. If we fix columns 2-3 and 4-5, then the remaining columns are 1 and 5? Wait, no, columns 1 is remaining, and column 5 is part of the fixed columns 4-5. Wait, columns 1 is remaining, column 4-5 are fixed. Wait, columns 2-3: fixed as C1, columns 4-5: fixed as C2. Therefore, remaining columns are 1. Column 1 has 2 cells, so 2^2=4. So |A2 \u2229 A4| = 2*2*4=16.\n\nSimilarly, (1,3) and (1,4) and (2,4) are all non-overlapping pairs with |Ai \u2229 Aj|=16.\n\nTherefore, for the pairs:\n\n- Overlapping pairs (1,2), (2,3), (3,4): each has |Ai \u2229 Aj|=32.\n\n- Non-overlapping pairs (1,3), (1,4), (2,4): each has |Ai \u2229 Aj|=16.\n\nTherefore, \u03a3|Ai \u2229 Aj| = 3*32 + 3*16 = 96 + 48 = 144.\n\nMoving on to the next term in inclusion-exclusion: \u03a3|Ai \u2229 Aj \u2229 Ak|. This is the sum over all triples of sets.\n\nFirst, how many triples are there? C(4,3)=4 triples. The triples are:\n\n(1,2,3), (1,2,4), (1,3,4), (2,3,4).\n\nNow, let's analyze each triple.\n\nFirst, (1,2,3): A1 \u2229 A2 \u2229 A3. This means all three 2x2 squares: columns 1-2, 2-3, and 3-4 are monochromatic. Let's see what constraints this imposes.\n\nFrom A1 and A2 overlapping, as before, columns 1-3 must be all the same color, say C1. Then from A3 (columns 3-4), which overlaps with A2 (columns 2-3). Since columns 3-4 must be monochromatic, and column 3 is already part of columns 2-3 (which are C1). Therefore, column 3 must be C1, so column 4 must also be C1. Therefore, columns 1-4 must all be C1, and column 5 is free. So columns 1-4 (each column's two cells) are color C1, column 5 is free (2 cells). So the number of colorings here is 2 (for C1) * 2^2 (column 5) = 2*4=8.\n\nSimilarly, (2,3,4): A2 \u2229 A3 \u2229 A4. This would require columns 2-5 to be all the same color. Wait, let's check:\n\nA2 is columns 2-3, A3 is columns 3-4, A4 is columns 4-5. Their union is columns 2-5. Following similar logic: columns 2-3 must be C1 (from A2), columns 3-4 must be C1 (from A3), columns 4-5 must be C1 (from A4). Therefore, columns 2-5 are all C1, and column 1 is free. So column 1 has 2 cells, 2^2=4. So number of colorings is 2*4=8.\n\nNow, the other triples: (1,2,4), (1,3,4).\n\nTake (1,2,4): A1 \u2229 A2 \u2229 A4. So A1 is columns 1-2, A2 is columns 2-3, A4 is columns 4-5. From A1 and A2, columns 1-3 must be C1. From A4, columns 4-5 must be C2. So columns 1-3: C1, columns 4-5: C2, column 3 is already C1, column 4 is C2. The remaining columns: none, since columns 1-3 and 4-5 cover all 5 columns. Wait, but columns 1-3 (C1) and 4-5 (C2). Therefore, the entire grid is colored with columns 1-3 as C1 and columns 4-5 as C2. So each column in 1-3 is C1, each column in 4-5 is C2. So how many colorings are there? For C1 and C2, each can be white or black, but they could be the same or different. Wait, no: A1 \u2229 A2 requires columns 1-3 to be monochromatic (either all white or all black), and A4 requires columns 4-5 to be monochromatic (either all white or all black). Therefore, the entire coloring is columns 1-3 as C1 and columns 4-5 as C2, where C1 and C2 can each be white or black independently. Therefore, the number of colorings is 2 (C1) * 2 (C2) = 4. Wait, but hold on. Let's verify:\n\nIf columns 1-3 are all C1, then each of those columns (1,2,3) must have both cells as C1. Similarly, columns 4-5 must be all C2. So all cells in columns 1-3 are C1, all cells in columns 4-5 are C2. There are 2 choices for C1 and 2 choices for C2, so total 2*2=4 colorings. Therefore, |A1 \u2229 A2 \u2229 A4| = 4.\n\nSimilarly, (1,3,4): A1 \u2229 A3 \u2229 A4. A1 is columns 1-2, A3 is columns 3-4, A4 is columns 4-5. So columns 1-2 must be C1, columns 3-4 must be C2, columns 4-5 must be C3. But columns 4 is part of both A3 and A4. Therefore, columns 4 must be both C2 and C3, so C2 = C3. Then columns 4-5 are C2. So columns 1-2: C1, columns 3-5: C2. Column 3 is C2, column 4-5 are C2. Therefore, columns 1-2: C1, columns 3-5: C2. So columns 1-2 (2 columns) and columns 3-5 (3 columns). Each of these column groups must be monochromatic. So number of colorings is 2 (C1) * 2 (C2) = 4. So |A1 \u2229 A3 \u2229 A4| = 4.\n\nSo the triples:\n\n- (1,2,3): 8\n\n- (2,3,4): 8\n\n- (1,2,4): 4\n\n- (1,3,4): 4\n\nTherefore, \u03a3|Ai \u2229 Aj \u2229 Ak| = 8 + 8 + 4 + 4 = 24.\n\nNow, the last term in inclusion-exclusion is |A1 \u2229 A2 \u2229 A3 \u2229 A4|. That is, all four 2x2 squares are monochromatic. Let's see what this implies.\n\nIf all four 2x2 squares (columns 1-2, 2-3, 3-4, 4-5) are monochromatic. Let's consider overlapping regions.\n\nStarting from the left: A1 (1-2) and A2 (2-3) must both be monochromatic. As before, this forces columns 1-3 to be the same color, say C1. Then A3 (3-4) must be monochromatic. Since column 3 is already C1, column 4 must also be C1. Then A4 (4-5) must be monochromatic. Since column 4 is C1, column 5 must also be C1. Therefore, all columns 1-5 must be color C1. So the entire grid is monochromatic (all white or all black). Therefore, the number of colorings is 2 (all white or all black). Therefore, |A1 \u2229 A2 \u2229 A3 \u2229 A4| = 2.\n\nNow, putting it all together:\n\nNumber of colorings with at least one monochromatic 2x2 square is:\n\n\u03a3|Ai| - \u03a3|Ai \u2229 Aj| + \u03a3|Ai \u2229 Aj \u2229 Ak| - |A1 \u2229 A2 \u2229 A3 \u2229 A4}| = 512 - 144 + 24 - 2 = 512 - 144 = 368; 368 +24= 392; 392 -2= 390.\n\nTherefore, the desired number of colorings is total colorings minus this: 1024 - 390 = 634.\n\nWait, but let me check the arithmetic again:\n\n512 (sum of |Ai|)\n\nminus 144 (sum of |Ai \u2229 Aj|) \u2192 512 - 144 = 368\n\nplus 24 (sum of |Ai \u2229 Aj \u2229 Ak|) \u2192 368 + 24 = 392\n\nminus 2 (sum of |A1 \u2229 ... \u2229 A4|) \u2192 392 - 2 = 390\n\nSo the inclusion-exclusion gives 390 colorings that have at least one monochromatic 2x2 square. Therefore, the valid colorings are 1024 - 390 = 634.\n\nBut wait, this seems a bit high. Let me check if my calculations for the intersections are correct.\n\nLet me verify the