isector**:\n   - Consider the external angle bisector of \\( A \\), which essentially forms the angle \\( \\angle x = 180^\\circ - 2 \\times \\frac{\\angle BAC}{2} = 180^\\circ - \\angle BAC \\).\n\n5. **Relating angles to prove parallelism**:\n   - From the external angle bisector theorem and circle properties, it can be shown that the line containing the external angle bisector equates to angle sums making it parallel to \\( MN \\).\n\n6. **Concluding parallelism**:\n   - The external angle bisector of angle \\( A \\) forms an angle that sits perfectly parallel to the chord \\( MN \\).\n   - Therefore, \\( MN \\parallel \\text{external bisector of } \\angle A \\).\n\nThis step-by-step approach matches geometric properties and relationships involved, concluding the solution effectively.\n\n\\(\\boxed{\\text{Conclusion: } MN \\parallel \\text{external bisector of } \\angle A }\\)", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Natural numbers from 1 to 2021 are written in a row in some order. It turned out that any number has neighbors with different parity. What number can be in the first place?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's tackle this problem step by step. So, we have natural numbers from 1 to 2021 arranged in a row in some order, and the condition is that any number has neighbors with different parity. We need to figure out what number can be in the first place. Hmm, parity means whether a number is even or odd, right? So, each number must be surrounded by numbers of the opposite parity. That means the sequence must alternate between even and odd numbers. \n\nWait, but the problem says \"any number has neighbors with different parity.\" So, for any position in the sequence, the number before and after it must be of the opposite parity. Therefore, the entire sequence must alternate between even and odd numbers. So, the arrangement must be either like even, odd, even, odd,... or odd, even, odd, even,... \