 breakdown:\n\n1. **Key Calendar Facts**:\n   - A common year (365 days) advances the day of the week by **1**.\n   - A leap year (366 days) advances it by **2**.\n   - The Gregorian calendar repeats every **400 years**, with **97 leap years** in this cycle.\n\n2. **Total Day Shifts Over 400 Years**:\n   - Total shifts = \\(400 \\text{ common years} \\times 1 + 97 \\text{ leap years} \\times 2 = 497 \\text{ days}\\).\n   - \\(497 \\mod 7 = 0\\), meaning the cycle resets perfectly every 400 years.\n\n3. **Distribution of New Year's Days**:\n   - In 400 years, there are \\(400\\) New Year's Days.\n   - Since \\(400 \\div 7 \\approx 57.14\\), one day occurs **58 times**, and the rest occur **57 times**.\n   - Due to the leap year rules and cumulative shifts, **Sunday, Wednesday, and Saturday** occur **58 times**, while other days occur **57 times**.\n\n4. **Given Date Context**:\n   - October 23, 1948, was a Saturday. Calculating forward, January 1, 1949, was also a **Saturday**.\n   - This aligns with the 400-year cycle where Saturday is one of the days occurring **58 times**.\n\n**Conclusion**: In the Gregorian calendar, **Sunday** occurs more frequently as New Year's Day than Monday. This conclusion holds regardless of specific start dates, as the 400-year cycle inherently favors Sunday, Wednesday, and Saturday. \n\n**Final Answer**:  \n\\boxed{Sunday}", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Determine the General Division of Days for Leap and Non-Leap Years:**\n   - A common year has 365 days, which consists of 52 weeks and 1 extra day:\n     \\[\n     365 = 52 \\times 7 + 1\n     \\]\n   - A leap year has 366 days, which consists of 52 weeks and 2 extra days:\n     \\[\n     366 = 52 \\times 7 + 2\n     \\]\n\n2. **Determine the New Year's Day Movement Without Centuries:**\n   - If we assume the starting year 0 is just after a leap year:\n     - New Year's Day Year 0 is day 0.\n     - New Year's Day Year 1 is day 1.\n     - New Year's Day Year 2 is day 2, and so on.\n  