C is formed by the intersection of chords BD and AC. The intercepted arcs would be arc BC and arc AD. The formula for the angle formed by two intersecting chords is that the angle is half the sum of the intercepted arcs. So, angle BEC = 1/2 (arc BC + arc AD). Since angle BEC is given as 130\u00b0, then:\n\n130\u00b0 = 1/2 (arc BC + arc AD)\n\nTherefore, arc BC + arc AD = 260\u00b0\n\nBut we already have that arc BC is equal to x (since AB = BC = CD), and arc DA is 360\u00b0 - 3x. Therefore:\n\narc BC + arc AD = x + (360\u00b0 - 3x) = 360\u00b0 - 2x = 260\u00b0\n\nSo, solving for x:\n\n360\u00b0 - 2x = 260\u00b0\n\nSubtract 260\u00b0 from both sides:\n\n100\u00b0 - 2x = 0\n\nWait, no, 360\u00b0 - 2x = 260\u00b0, so subtract 260\u00b0 from 360\u00b0:\n\n360\u00b0 - 260\u00b0 = 2x\n\n100\u00b0 = 2x\n\nTherefore, x = 50\u00b0\n\nSo, each of the arcs AB, BC, CD is 50\u00b0, and arc DA is 360\u00b0 - 3*50\u00b0 = 360\u00b0 - 150\u00b0 = 210\u00b0\n\nOkay, so arcs:\n\nAB = 50\u00b0, BC = 50\u00b0, CD = 50\u00b0, DA = 210\u00b0\n\nNow, we need to find angle ACD. Angle ACD is an angle at point C, formed by points A, C, D. So, angle at C between points A and D. In circle geometry, the measure of an inscribed angle is half the measure of its intercepted arc. So, angle ACD intercepts arc AD. Therefore, angle ACD = 1/2 arc AD.\n\nSince arc AD is 210\u00b0, angle ACD would be 1/2 * 210\u00b0 = 105\u00b0. Hmm, that's one of the options, option D. But let me verify this carefully.\n\nWait, angle ACD is at point C, and it's formed by chords AC and CD. The intercepted arc would be arc AD. So yes, the inscribed angle theorem says that angle ACD is half the measure of arc AD. Since arc AD is 210\u00b0, then angle ACD is 105\u00b0, so the answer is D.\n\nBut let me check again because sometimes angles can be subtended from different arcs. Wait, angle at C, between A and D. Since points A and D are on the circumference, and C is also on the circumference, angle ACD is indeed an inscribed