h test values, the final solution set for the inequality \\( f(x) < 6 \\) is \\(\\boxed{(0, 4)}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Given that \\( f(x) \\) is a monotonic function defined on the interval \\( (0, +\\infty) \\), we have the functional equation:\n   \\[\n   f\\left(f(x) - 2 \\log_2 x\\right) = 4\n   \\]\n   for all \\( x \\in (0, +\\infty) \\).\n\n2. Let's denote by \\( c \\) a constant value such that \\( f(c) = 4 \\). We need to find this \\( c \\).\n\n3. Substitute \\( x = c \\) in the given functional equation:\n   \\[\n   f\\left(f(c) - 2 \\log_2 c\\right) = 4\n   \\]\n   Since \\( f(c) = 4 \\), the equation becomes:\n   \\[\n   f(4 - 2 \\log_2 c) = 4\n   \\]\n\n4. By the monotonicity of \\( f \\), the equation \\( f(u) = 4 \\) has a unique solution \\( u = c \\). Hence:\n   \\[\n   4 - 2 \\log_2 c = c\n   \\]\n\n5. Solve the equation \\( 4 - 2 \\log_2 c = c \\) for \\( c \\):\n   \\[\n   c + 2 \\log_2 c = 4\n   \\]\n\n6. By observation or trial, we find that \\( c = 2 \\) satisfies the equation:\n   \\[\n   2 + 2 \\log_2 2 = 2 + 2 \\cdot 1 = 4\n   \\]\n   Thus, \\( c = 2 \\).\n\n7. Given \\( c = 2 \\) and \\( f(c) = 4 \\), we can substitute back to express \\( f(x) \\) in terms of \\( x \\):\n   \\[\n   f(x) = 2 \\log_2 x + 2\n   \\]\n\n8. We need to find the solution set where \\( f(x) < 6 \\):\n   \\[\n   f(x) < 6\n   \\]\n\n9. Substitute \\( f(x) = 2 \\log_2 x + 2 \\) into the inequality:\n   \\[\n   2 \\log_2 x + 2 < 6\n   \\]\n\n10. Simplify the inequality:\n    \\[\n    2 \\log_2 x + 2 < 6 \\implies 2 \\log_2 x < 4 \\implies \\log_2 x < 2\n    \\]\n\n11. Convert the logarithmic inequality to its exponential form:\n    \\[\n    \\log_2 x < 2 \\implies x < 2^2 \\implies x < 4\n    \\]\n\n12. Given \\( x \\in (0, +\\infty) \\), the solution set for \\( f(x) < 6 \\) is:\n    \\[\n    0 < x < 4\n    \\]\n\nConclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{x \\mid 0 < x < 4\\}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": nul