60x + 225. Wait, but let's check if this works.\n\nSo, if we subtract the line from the quartic, we should get (x\u00b2 - 2x -15)^2. Let's compute (x\u00b2 - 2x -15)^2:\n\n= x^4 -4x\u00b3 + (4 - 30)x\u00b2 + 60x + 225\n\nWait, wait, let's actually compute it step by step:\n\n(x\u00b2 - 2x -15)(x\u00b2 - 2x -15) = x\u00b2(x\u00b2 -2x -15) -2x(x\u00b2 -2x -15) -15(x\u00b2 -2x -15)\n\n= x\u2074 -2x\u00b3 -15x\u00b2 -2x\u00b3 +4x\u00b2 +30x -15x\u00b2 +30x +225\n\nCombine like terms:\n\nx\u2074 + (-2x\u00b3 -2x\u00b3) + (-15x\u00b2 +4x\u00b2 -15x\u00b2) + (30x +30x) +225\n\n= x\u2074 -4x\u00b3 -26x\u00b2 +60x +225.\n\nBut the original quartic minus the line is x\u2074 -4x\u00b3 -26x\u00b2 -mx -b. So if we have x\u2074 -4x\u00b3 -26x\u00b2 +60x +225. Therefore, comparing with x\u2074 -4x\u00b3 -26x\u00b2 -mx -b, we have:\n\n+60x +225 = -mx -b \u21d2 m = -60 and b = -225. Hence, the line is y = -60x - (-225) \u21d2 y = -60x + 225.\n\nBut wait, when we subtract the line, we get (x\u00b2 -2x -15)^2. Since a square is always non-negative, this would mean the quartic lies above the line y = -60x +225 for all x, because (x\u00b2 -2x -15)^2 \u22650. So, y_quartic - y_line \u22650 for all x. Therefore, the quartic lies on or above the line, and touches the line at the roots of x\u00b2 -2x -15 =0, which are x = [2 \u00b1 sqrt(4 +60)]/2 = [2 \u00b1 sqrt(64)]/2 = [2 \u00b18]/2. So, x = (2 +8)/2 = 5, x = (2 -8)/2 = -3. Therefore, the quartic touches the line at x =5 and x=-3. So, two points of contact. Since the square is non-negative, the quartic is always above or on the line. So the line is y = -60x +225, and the quartic lies above it, touching at x=-3 and x=5.\n\nTherefore, that seems to satisfy the problem's condition. Let me verify by plugging in x=-3 and x=5 into both the quartic and the line.\n\nFirst, at x = -3:\n\nQuartic: (-3)^4 -4*(-3)^3 -26*(-3)^2 = 81 -4*(-27) -26*9 = 81 +108 -234 = (81 +108) -234 = 189 -234 = -45.\n\nLine: y = -60*(-3) +225 = 180 +225 = 405. Wait, tha