ly one n such that n\u00b3 \u2261 m\u00b2 -1 mod p. Therefore, the total number of solutions is p-1 (since m ranges from 1 to p-1). Wait, but this contradicts our previous result of p-3.\n\nWhat's going on here? If for each m, there is exactly one n such that n\u00b3 \u2261 m\u00b2 -1 mod p, because the cube map is a bijection, then the total number of solutions would be p-1. But this can't be right because when m\u00b2 -1 \u2261 -1 mod p, then n\u00b3 \u2261 -1 mod p has a solution, but m\u00b2 -1 \u2261 -1 implies m\u00b2 \u2261 0 mod p, which implies m=0, but m ranges from 1 to p-1. Therefore, there is no m with m\u00b2 \u2261 0 mod p. Therefore, the equation n\u00b3 \u2261 m\u00b2 -1 mod p has solutions for all m except when m\u00b2 \u2261 1 mod p? Wait, no, if the cube map is a bijection, then for every element in F_p*, there is exactly one n such that n\u00b3 \u2261 element. So for each m from 1 to p-1, m\u00b2 -1 is in F_p (since m\u00b2 is from 1 to (p-1)^2 mod p, but m\u00b2 is a quadratic residue). So if m\u00b2 -1 is in F_p*, then there's exactly one n such that n\u00b3 \u2261 m\u00b2 -1. If m\u00b2 -1 \u2261 0 mod p, which would require m\u00b2 \u22611 mod p. The solutions to m\u00b2 \u22611 mod p are m=1 and m=p-1. Therefore, for m=1 and m=p-1, m\u00b2 -1 \u22610 mod p, so n\u00b3 \u22610 mod p, which would require n=0, but n ranges from 1 to p-1. Therefore, no solution for these two m's. Therefore, the total number of solutions is (p-1) -2 = p-3, which matches our previous count. Therefore, by fixing m and solving for n, we have p-3 solutions, which is at most p-1. Therefore, the number of members of the set divisible by p is at most p-1.\n\nAh, this is a cleaner argument. Here's the step-by-step:\n\n1. Since p \u22612 mod 3, the cube map n \u21a6 n\u00b3 mod p is a permutation of F_p*. Therefore, for any a \u2208 F_p*, there exists a unique n \u2208 F_p* such that n\u00b3 \u2261 a mod p.\n\n2. Consider the equation m\u00b2 \u2261 n\u00b3 +1 mod p. Rearranged, this is n\u00b3 \u2261 m\u00b2 -1 mod p.\n\n3. For each m \u2208 F_p*, there is a unique n \u2208 F_p* such that n\u00b3 \u2261 m\u00b2 -1 mod p, provided that m\u00b2 -1 \u22600 mod p.\n\n4. The values of m for which m\u00b2 -1 \u22610 mod p are m \u22611 and m \u2261-1 mod p. Since m \u2208 F_p* (0 < m < p), these correspond to m=1 and m=p-1.\n\n5. For these two values of m (1 and p-1), the equation n\u00b3 \u22610 mod p has no solution in F_p*, so no corresponding n exists.\n\n6. Therefore, for the remaining p-3 values of m (excluding m=1 and m=p-1), there exists a unique n \u2208 F_p* such that n\u00b3 \u2261 m\u00b2 -1 mod p.\n\n7. Each such pair (m, n) corresponds to an element m\u00b2 -n\u00b3 -1 \u22610 mod p.\n\n8. Thus, there are exactly p-3 such pairs (m, n) where m and n are in F_p*.\n\n9. Each pair (m, n) produces a distinct element m\u00b2 -n\u00b3 -1 in the set {m\u00b2 -n\u00b3 -1 | 0 < m, n < p}, which is divisible by p.\n\n10. Therefore, there are exactly p-3 elements in the set divisible by p.\n\n11. Since p-3 \u2264 p-1, the conclusion is that at most p-1 members of the set are divisible by p.\n\nThis completes the proof. The key step is recognizing that the cube map is a bijection, which allows us to pair each m (except two) with a unique n, resulting in p-3 solutions. Since p-3 is less than or equal to p-1, the bound holds.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that at most \\( p-1 \\) members of the set \\( \\{m^2 - n^3 - 1 \\;|\\; 0 < m, n < p\\} \\) are divisible by \\( p \\), we proceed as follows:\n\n1. **Bijection of the Cube Map**: Since \\( p \\equiv 2 \\mod 3 \\), \\( 3 \\) and \\( p-1 \\) are coprime. Thus, the map \\( n \\mapsto n^3 \\mod p \\) is a permutation of \\( \\mathbb{F}_p^* \\). Every non-zero residue modulo \\( p \\) is a cube.\n\n2. **Equation Rearrangement**: The equation \\( m^2 \\equiv n^3 + 1 \\mod p \\) can be rewritten as \\( n^3 \\equiv m^2 - 1 \\mod p \\). For each \\( m \\in \\mathbb{F}_p^* \\), there exists a unique \\( n \\in \\mathbb{F}_p^* \\) such that \\( n^3 \\equiv m^2 - 1 \\mod p \\), provided \\( m^2 - 1 \\not\\equiv 0 \\mod p \\).\n\n3. **Exceptional Cases**: The equation \\( m^2 \\equiv 1 \\mod p \\) has solutions \\( m \\equiv \\pm 1 \\mod p \\). For \\( m = 1 \\) and \\( m = p-1 \\), \\( m^2 - 1 \\equiv 0 \\mod p \\), which implies \\( n^3 \\equiv 0 \\mod p \\). Since \\( n \\in \\mathbb{F}_p^* \\), no such \\( n \\) exists for these two values of \\( m \\).\n\n4. **Counting Solutions**: Excluding \\( m = 1 \\) and \\( m = p-1 \\), there are \\( p-3 \\) remaining values of \\( m \\in \\mathbb{F}_p^* \\). For each of these \\( m \\), there is a unique \\( n \\in \\mathbb{F}_p^* \\) satisfying \\( n^3 \\equiv m^2 - 1 \\mod p \\). This results in \\( p-3 \\) distinct pairs \\( (m, n) \\).\n\n5. **Distinct Elements in the Set**: Each valid pair \\( (m, n) \\) produces an element \\( m^2 - n^3 - 1 \\equiv 0 \\mod p \\). Since these pairs are distinct and \\( m, n \\) are constrained to \\( \\mathbb{F}_p^* \\), the number of distinct elements in the set divisible by \\( p \\) is exactly \\( p-3 \\).\n\n6. **Final Bound**: Since \\( p-3 \\leq p-1 \\), the number of such elements is at most \\( p-1 \\).\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, at most \\( p-1 \\) members of the set \\( \\{m^2 - n^3 - 1 \\;|\\; 0 < m, n < p\\} \\) are divisible by \\( p \\).\n\n\\(\\boxed{\\text{At most } p-1 \\text{ members of the set are divisible by } p.}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "Given:\n- \\( p \\) is an odd prime such that \\( p \\equiv 2 \\pmod{3} \\).\n- We need to prove that at most \\( p-1 \\) members of the set \\(\\{ m^2 - n^3 - 1: 0 < m, n < p \\}\\) are divisible by \\( p \\).\n\n1. **Step 1: Properties of \\( p \\).**\n    Given that \\( p \\) is an odd prime and \\( p \\equiv 2 \\pmod{3} \\), one significant result is that \\( p \\) has a primitive root. Let \\( k \\) be such a primitive root of \\( p \\). This means any non-zero residue modulo \\( p \\) can be expressed as \\( k^a \\mod p \\) for \\( a \\) in the range \\( 1, 2, \\ldots, p-1 \\).\n    \n2. **Step 2: Distinctness of cubic residues.**\n    We need to show that \\( p \\) has \\( p-1 \\) distinct cubic residues modulo \\( p \\).\n\n    Let \\( k \\) be a primitive root of \\( p \\). Then, any non-zero element modulo \\( p \\) can be written as \\( k^a \\mod p \\) for \\( a \\) in \\( \\{1, 2, \\ldots, p-1\\} \\). Therefore, if we consider \\( k^{3a} \\mod p \\) for \\( a \\) in this range, these residues should be distinct.\n\n    If \\( k^{3a} \\equiv k^{3b} \\pmod{p} \\) for some \\( a \\) and \\( b \\), then:\n    \\[\n    k^{3a} \\equiv k^{3b} \\pmod{p} \\Rightarrow k^{3(a-b)} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{p}\n    \\]\n    Given that \\( k \\) is a primitive root and the order of \\( k \\) is \\( p-1 \\), we have:\n    \\[\n    k^{p-1} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{p}\n    \\]\n    For \\( k^{3(a-b)} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{p} \\) to hold, \\( 3(a-b) \\) must be a multiple of \\( p-1 \\). Since \\( p \\equiv 2 \\pmod{3} \\), \\( p-1 \\) is not a multiple of 3. Therefore, \\( p-1 \\) must divide \\( a - b \\). But since \\( |a - b| < p-1 \\) and \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are distinct, this cannot happen. Hence, \\( k^{3a} \\) and \\( k^{3b} \\) are distinct for \\( a \\ne b \\).\n\n3. **Step 3: Considering the Set \\(\\{ m^2 - n^3 - 1 \\mod p: 0 < m, n < p \\}\\).**\n    For a fixed \\( m \\) in the range \\( 1, 2, \\ldots, p-1 \\), consider values of the form \\( m^2 - n^3 - 1 \\mod p \\).\n\n    Since \\( n^3 \\) ranges over all \\( p-1 \\) residues and they are distinct modulo \\( p \\), the sequence \\( m^2 - n^3 - 1 \\) also consists of \\( p-1 \\) distinct residues modulo \\( p \\). As these residues are distinct modulo \\( p \\), at most one of them can be \\( 0 \\pmod{p} \\).\n\n    Therefore, for each \\( m \\) in \\( 1, 2, \\ldots, p-1 \\), at most one value of \\( n \\) in \\( 1, 2, \\ldots, p-1 \\) will make \\( m^2 - n^3 - 1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{p} \\).\n\n4. **Conclusion:**\n    Since there are \\( p-1 \\) possible values for \\( m \\) and for each \\( m \\), at most one \\( n \\) makes \\( m^2 - n^3 - 1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{p} \\), we conclude that at most \\( p-1 \\) elements of the set \\(\\{ m^2 - n^3 - 1 : 0 < m, n < p \\}\\) are divisible by \\( p \\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{p-1}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "100 prisoners are placed in a prison. The warden tells them:\n\n\"I will give you an evening to talk with each other, and then I will place you in separate cells, and you will no longer be able to communicate. Occasionally, I will take one of you to a room that contains a lamp (initially turned off). When you leave the room, you can leave the lamp either on or off.\n\nIf at some point one of you tells me that all of you have already been in the room and it is true, then I will set you all free. If you are wrong, I will feed you all to the crocodiles. Don't worry about anyone being forgotten - if you remain silent, then everyone will eventually visit the room, and no one's visit to the room will be their last.\"\n\nDevise a strategy that guarantees the prisoners' release.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out a strategy for the 100 prisoners so that one of them can eventually tell the warden that everyone has been in the room, and they can all be set free. Let me start by understanding the problem again.\n\nThere are 100 prisoners. They get a chance to discuss a strategy one evening, then they're put in separate cells and can't communicate anymore. The warden will occasionally take one prisoner to a room with a lamp that's initially off. Each time a prisoner leaves the room, they can choose to leave the lamp on or off. The goal is that at some point, a prisoner can correctly declare that everyone has been in the room, and if they're right, they go free; if not, they get fed to crocodiles. The warden assures them that everyone will eventually visit the room infi