s counted 99 others, but he himself has been in the room (as per the warden's promise that everyone visits infinitely often), then he knows that he has been there, so when he counts 99, he knows all 100 have been there. But does he need to count himself?\n\nWait, maybe there's a confusion here. Let's clarify. The leader is one of the 100 prisoners. To have all 100 prisoners have been in the room, the leader must have been there, and the 99 others must have been there. However, the leader's count is only for the 99 others. So when the leader's count reaches 99, he knows that the other 99 have been there. But he himself, as a prisoner, must have been in the room at least once (as per the warden's statement that everyone will visit the room infinitely often if they remain silent). However, the leader doesn't need to count himself because he's the one making the declaration. So once he's counted 99, he can declare, because he himself has been in the room (since he's alive and making the declaration), so all 100 have been there. Wait, no. Because the leader could potentially be the first one to be taken to the room, before any others. But in that case, he would have a count of 0. But according to the warden, if they remain silent, everyone will eventually visit. So the leader's count will eventually reach 99. But the problem is that the leader can only make the declaration when he is taken to the room. So when the leader is taken to the room and his count is 99, he can declare. At that point, since he's been taken to the room (so he has been there), and the count is 99 others, that's 100 total. Therefore, the strategy works.\n\nBut let me check for possible errors. Suppose a non-leader is taken multiple times before the leader can count their signal. For example, non-leader A is taken, turns the lamp on. Then non-leader A is taken again, sees the lamp on, but does nothing (since they already turned it on once). Then the leader is taken, turns it off, count=1. That's okay