mp is on, they will have to wait until a subsequent visit when it's off to turn it on. But the leader is turning it off whenever he sees it on. So if the lamp is on, the leader will eventually turn it off. Then, any non-leader who hasn't turned it on yet can do so when they see it off.\n\nThis could lead to a problem where a non-leader might never get a chance to turn the lamp on if the leader is too slow. Wait, but the warden's condition is that if they remain silent, everyone will visit the room infinitely often. Therefore, every non-leader will have infinitely many chances to enter the room. So even if their first few visits are when the lamp is on, eventually, they will be taken when the lamp is off (since the leader is turning it off each time he sees it on, and the lamp can't stay on forever if the leader is taken infinitely often). Therefore, every non-leader will eventually have an opportunity to turn the lamp on, which the leader will count.\n\nTherefore, the strategy is robust. Each non-leader will eventually get a chance to turn the lamp on once, and the leader will count each of those. Once the leader has counted 99, he can declare success.\n\nSo the answer is to designate one prisoner as the counter. The counter's role is to turn the lamp off every time they find it on, incrementing their count each time. The other prisoners, upon entering the room for the first time when the lamp is off, turn it on. Once the counter has turned the lamp off 99 times, they can declare that all prisoners have visited the room.", "deepseek_solution": "To ensure the prisoners' release, they can adopt the following strategy:\n\n1. **Designate a Leader (Counter):** One prisoner is chosen as the leader. Their role is to count the number of times the lamp is turned on by others.\n\n2. **Non-Leader Strategy:** Every other prisoner (non-leader) follows this rule:\n   - If they enter the room and **the lamp is off** and they **have never turned it on before**, they turn it on.\n   - In all other cases, they leave the lamp as they found it (whether on or off).\n\n3. **Leader's Action:** The leader adheres to this rule:\n   - Every time they find the lamp **on**, they turn it **off** and increment their count by 1.\n   - Once their count reaches **99**, they declare that all prisoners have visited the room.\n\n**How It Works:**\n- Each non-leader signals their first visit by turning the lamp on once (and only once). The leader tracks these signals by turning the lamp off and counting. \n- Since the warden guarantees infinite visits, every non-leader will eventually encounter the lamp off and trigger their single signal. \n- After 99 signals, the leader knows all 99 non-leaders (and themselves) have visited, ensuring the declaration is correct.\n\n**Final Answer:**\nThe prisoners should designate one leader. Non-leaders turn the lamp on once (only when off and they haven't done so before). The leader counts each time they turn the lamp off. When the count reaches 99, the leader declares all have visited, guaranteeing release.", "ground_truth_solution": "### Problem:\n100 prisoners are locked in separate cells with no ability to communicate with each other. There is a room with a light, initially turned off, which each prisoner will be taken to in a random order. Each prisoner can toggle the light switch once they are in the room. If any prisoner can eventually say that all 100 prisoners have been in the room at least once, they all will be set free. If the guess is wrong, they all will be fed to crocodiles.\n\n1. **Selection of a Counter:**\n   The prisoners will select one specific prisoner (we will call him the \"counter\") who will be responsible for counting the prisoners who have visited the room.\n\n2. **Light as a Signal:**\n   The counter will turn off the light whenever he finds it turned on. Therefore, the state of the light bulb (on or off) becomes a signal to the counter. The light being on signifies that another prisoner has visited the room and left it on.\n\n3. **Initial Counting by the Counter:**\n   - When the counter enters the room and finds the light off, he does nothing.\n   - When the counter enters the room and finds the light on, he turns it off and increments his count by one.\n\n4. **Actions of the Other Prisoners:**\n   - If a prisoner enters the room and finds the light off, and if they have never turned it on before, they turn the light on. \n   - If the light is already on or they have already turned the light on once before, they do nothing and just leave the light as it is.\n\n5. **Counting Until 99:**\n   - The counter will continue to count until he has turned off the light 99 times. \n   - Counting to 99 ensures that all prisoners have visited the room since there are 100 prisoners in total, and the counter himself does not need to be counted by another prisoner.\n\n6. **Final Decision by the Counter:**\n   - Once the counter has counted to 99, he concludes that every prisoner must have been in the room at least once. He can then announce that all prisoners have visited the room, thus securing their freedom.\n\n7. **Verification of the Strategy:**\n   - Assume it is the case that one of the prisoners has never turned on the light switch at least once. This prisoner will eventually enter the room after the nth daylight where \\( n < 100 \\).\n   - As entering the room does not prohibit further visits, every prisoner will definitely have the opportunity to enter and turn on the light once ensuring the counter's increments proceed accurately until they reach up to 99.\n\nConclusion:\n\\[\\boxed{\\text{All prisoners will be set free following this strategy.}}\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Squares \\( A B K L, B C M N, C A O P \\) are drawn externally on the sides of a triangle \\( A B C \\). The line segments \\( K L, M N, O P \\), when extended, form a triangle \\( A^{\\prime} B^{\\prime} C^{\\prime} 