imes \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\sqrt{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Since these triangles appear at each vertex of \\(\\triangle ABC\\), there are three such triangles in total contributing to the area:\n   \\[\n   3 \\times \\sqrt{3} = 3\\sqrt{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Determine the Area of the Triangle formed by \\(KL\\), \\(MN\\), and \\(OP\\):**\n\n   The outer regions, which form \\(\\triangle A'B'C'\\), are considered next. It is established that these regions outside the original triangle linking each square contribute very specific regions, which are essentially three larger equilateral triangles. These have side lengths of \\(2\\sqrt{3}\\) each.\n\n   The area of a single large equilateral triangle with side length \\(2\\sqrt{3}\\):\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area}_{\\triangle} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\times (2\\sqrt{3})^2 = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\times 12 = 3\\sqrt{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Since there are three parts combined from extensions, additional areas:\n   \\[\n   3 \\times 3\\sqrt{3} = 9\\sqrt{3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Total Area of \\(\\triangle A'B'C'\\):**\n\n   Summarizing the above calculations:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area}_{A'B'C'} = \\text{Area}_{\\triangle ABC} + \\text{Area}_{\\text{squares}} +  \\text{Area Aggregate} \\]\n   Summed in units:\n   \\[ \n   = \\sqrt{3} + 12 + 3\\sqrt{3} + 9\\sqrt{3} = 12 + 13\\sqrt{3}\n   \\]\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{12 + 13\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]\n", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Prove that \\(2^{p-1} (2^p - 1)\\) is a perfect number when \\(2^p - 1\\) is prime. \n\n(A perfect number equals twice the sum of its positive divisors, excluding the number itself.)", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that \\(2^{p-1} (2^p - 1)\\) is a perfect number when \\(2^p - 1\\) is prime. Hmm, let's start by recalling what a perfect number is. The problem statement says a perfect number equals twice the sum of its positive divisors excluding the number itself. So, if I denote the number as