.5 times bigger, which is an increase. So, even though both are decreased, since the denominator is decreased more, the overall effect is an increase in the fraction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is (d), increases by 50%.", "deepseek_solution": "When the numerator \\( x \\) is decreased by 40%, it becomes \\( 0.6x \\). When the denominator \\( y \\) is decreased by 60%, it becomes \\( 0.4y \\). The new fraction is:  \n\\[\n\\frac{0.6x}{0.4y} = \\frac{0.6}{0.4} \\cdot \\frac{x}{y} = 1.5 \\cdot \\frac{x}{y}.\n\\]  \nThis shows the fraction becomes \\( 1.5 \\) times the original, equivalent to a **50% increase**.  \n\n**Verification with Example**:  \nLet \\( x = 100 \\) and \\( y = 100 \\). Original fraction: \\( \\frac{100}{100} = 1 \\).  \nNew numerator: \\( 100 - 40\\% \\times 100 = 60 \\).  \nNew denominator: \\( 100 - 60\\% \\times 100 = 40 \\).  \nNew fraction: \\( \\frac{60}{40} = 1.5 \\), confirming a 50% increase.  \n\n**Answer**: (d) increases by 50%.", "ground_truth_solution": "1. To solve this problem, first determine the new values of the numerator \\( x \\) and denominator \\( y \\) after the respective percentage decreases.\n\n2. Let's start by decreasing the numerator \\( x \\) by \\( 40\\% \\):\n   \\[\n   \\text{New numerator} = x - \\left(0.40 \\times x\\right) = x \\left(1 - 0.40\\right) = 0.60x = 0.6x\n   \\]\n\n3. Next, decrease the denominator \\( y \\) by \\( 60\\% \\):\n   \\[\n   \\text{New denominator} = y - \\left(0.60 \\times y\\right) = y \\left(1 - 0.60\\right) = 0.40y = 0.4y\n   \\]\n\n4. The new fraction becomes:\n   \\[\n   \\text{New fraction} = \\frac{0.6x}{0.4y}\n   \\]\n\n5. Simplify the fraction by dividing both the numerator and the denominator by \\( 0.4 \\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{0.6x}{0.4y} = \\frac{0.6}{0.4} \\times \\frac{x}{y} = \\frac{6}{4} \\times \\frac{x}{y} = 1.5 \\times \\frac{x}{y}\n   \\]\n\n6. The new value of the fraction is \\( 1.5 \\times \\frac{x}{y} \\), which means the fraction \\( \\frac{x}{y} \\) has incr