 then both sides must be non-negative. The left side requires \\(a \\geq b - c\\) and \\(b \\geq c\\). The right side is \\(a - b + c\\). For \\(a - b + c\\) to be non-negative, we need \\(a + c \\geq b\\), which is the same as the left side's condition. Therefore, the conditions are \\(b \\geq c\\) and \\(a + c \\geq b\\). But since \\(a + c \\geq b\\) already implies \\(b - c \\leq a\\), which might not necessarily require \\(b \\geq c\\). Wait, no. If \\(a + c \\geq b\\), even if \\(b > c\\), as long as \\(a\\) is sufficiently large. Wait, but \\(b - c\\) must be non-negative because we are in a context without negative numbers. So first, \\(b \\geq c\\) is necessary for \\(b - c\\) to be allowed. Then, \\(a \\geq b - c\\) is needed for \\(a - (b - c)\\) to be non-negative. Therefore, the two conditions: \\(b \\geq c\\) and \\(a \\geq b - c\\). But since \\(a\\) is non-negative, and \\(b - c\\) is non-negative (due to \\(b \\geq c\\)), so \\(a\\) must be at least \\(b - c\\). So combined, the conditions are \\(b \\geq c\\) and \\(a \\geq b - c\\). Alternatively, \\(a + c \\geq b\\) if we rearrange. Wait, \\(a \\geq b - c\\) is equivalent to \\(a + c \\geq b\\). So maybe the condition can be written as \\(a + c \\geq b\\). But we also need to have \\(b \\geq c\\) to define \\(b - c\\). So actually, both \\(b \\geq c\\) and \\(a + c \\geq b\\) are necessary. However, if \\(a + c \\geq b\\) is true, and since \\(a\\) is non-negative, then even if \\(b > c\\), as long as \\(a\\) compensates. But wait, but \\(b - c\\) has to be a valid subtraction (non-negative). So even if \\(a + c \\geq b\\), if \\(b > c\\), the subtraction \\(b - c\\) is allowed only if \\(b \\geq c\\). Therefore, the two separate conditions: \\(b \\geq c\\) and \\(a + c \\geq b\\). So both must hold.\n\n6) \\((a + b)(c - d) = ac + bc - ad - bd\\). Let's break this down. The left side has \\(c - d\\), so we need \\(c \\geq d\\). Then, the product \\((a + b)(c - d)\\) would be non-negative if \\(a +