b\\), \\(-d(a - b)\\) is non-positive. Wait, but the right side is \\(ac - bc - ad + bd\\), which combines these. The entire expression is allowed to be negative? But in the context before negative numbers, the entire expression must be non-negative. Wait, but if \\((a - b)(c - d)\\) is non-negative, then the right side must also be non-negative. So to ensure that \\((a - b)(c - d) \\geq 0\\), given that \\(a \\geq b\\) and \\(c \\geq d\\), the product is non-negative. Therefore, the conditions are \\(a \\geq b\\) and \\(c \\geq d\\). However, even if both \\(a \\geq b\\) and \\(c \\geq d\\), their product is non-negative. So the formula holds, but the result is non-negative as long as both factors are non-negative. Therefore, the conditions are \\(a \\geq b\\) and \\(c \\geq d\\).\n\nSo summarizing each formula's conditions:\n\n1) \\(b \\geq c\\)\n\n2) \\(a \\geq b + c\\)\n\n3) \\(b \\geq c\\)\n\n4) \\(b \\geq c\\)\n\n5) \\(b \\geq c\\) and \\(a + c \\geq b\\) (which simplifies to \\(a \\geq b - c\\), but since \\(b \\geq c\\), \\(b - c\\) is non-negative, so \\(a\\) must be at least that)\n\n6) \\(c \\geq d\\)\n\n7) \\(a \\geq b\\) and \\(c \\geq d\\)\n\nLet me double-check formula 5 again. The left side is \\(a - (b - c)\\). To compute this, first \\(b - c\\) must be non-negative (so \\(b \\geq c\\)), and then \\(a\\) must be at least \\(b - c\\) to make \\(a - (b - c)\\) non-negative. So the conditions are indeed \\(b \\geq c\\) and \\(a \\geq b - c\\). But since \\(a\\) is non-negative, and \\(b - c\\) is non-negative (from \\(b \\geq c\\)), \\(a \\geq b - c\\) is necessary. Alternatively, since \\(a - (b - c) = a - b + c\\), which is \\( (a + c) - b \\), so to have this non-negative, \\(a + c \\geq b\\). So another way to write the condition is \\(a + c \\geq b\\), but we also need \\(b \\geq c\\) to have \\(b - c\\) defined. Wait, but \\(a + c \\geq b\\) might automatically imply \\(b - c \\leq a\\), but does it imply \\(b \\geq c\\)? Suppose \\(a + c \\geq b