PT2; these are the two tangents from P to circle O(d).\n\nThese lines PT1 and PT2 are the desired secants that intersect the original circle O(r) at points A and B, with AB = 2a.\n\nBut let's verify this with an example. Suppose P is outside the original circle O(r). Then, the circle O(d) is smaller, so if P is outside O(d), we can draw two tangents. Each tangent touches O(d) at a point, say T1 and T2. Then, the lines PT1 and PT2 pass through P and are tangent to O(d). These lines intersect the original circle O(r) at points A and B. The chord AB lies along the tangent line, and since the distance from O to AB is d, the length of AB is 2a.\n\nYes, this makes sense. Therefore, the construction is valid.\n\nBut what if P is inside the original circle? Then, the circle O(d) has radius d = \u221a(r\u00b2 - a\u00b2). If P is inside O(r), depending on the value of d, P could be inside or outside O(d). For example, if a is small, d is close to r, so O(d) is a circle almost as large as the original. If P is inside O(r), then if d > OP, then P is inside O(d); if d < OP, then P is outside O(d). Wait, let's see:\n\nOP is the distance from O to P. The circle O(d) has radius d. So:\n\n- If OP > d, then P is outside O(d).\n\n- If OP = d, then P is on O(d).\n\n- If OP < d, then P is inside O(d).\n\nTherefore, if P is inside the original circle O(r), whether it's inside or outside O(d) depends on the comparison between OP and d = \u221a(r\u00b2 - a\u00b2).\n\nTherefore, if OP > \u221a(r\u00b2 - a\u00b2), then P is outside O(d), so we can draw two tangents from P to O(d). These tangents will be the secant lines passing through P and intersecting O(r) at AB with length 2a.\n\nIf OP = \u221a(r\u00b2 - a\u00b2), then P is on O(d), so there's one tangent, which is the line perpendicular to OP at P. But since P is on O(d), the tangent line at P to O(d) is perpendicular to OP. This line will intersect O(r) at two points A and B such that AB is the chord of length 2a.\n\nIf OP < \u221a(r\u