d the minimal a such that the covering radius is exactly 1, which would give the equality a = cos \u03b1 + \u221a3 sin \u03b1. Then, for a less than or equal to that, the covering radius is \u22641, hence the circles cover the parallelogram.\n\nSo, how to compute the covering radius? The covering radius here is the maximum over all points in the parallelogram of the minimal distance to the four vertices. Therefore, to find the maximal minimal distance, we need to find the point(s) in the parallelogram where the distance to all four vertices is maximized. The maximum of these minimal distances is the covering radius, and we need this to be \u22641.\n\nTherefore, the strategy is to find the point P in the parallelogram ABCD such that the minimal distance from P to A, B, C, D is maximized. Then, set this maximum minimal distance to 1 and solve for a in terms of \u03b1.\n\nAlternatively, since the parallelogram is convex and symmetric, the point with the maximal minimal distance might be located at the center of the parallelogram or somewhere along the edges or diagonals.\n\nAlternatively, the farthest point from all vertices could be the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of the sides or something like that. But in a parallelogram, the center is the midpoint of the diagonals, so let's check the distances from the center.\n\nLet\u2019s compute the coordinates of the center, which is the midpoint of diagonal AC (and also BD). The midpoint M has coordinates ((a cos \u03b1 + 1)/2, (a sin \u03b1)/2). The distance from M to each vertex is half the length of the diagonal. So, distance from M to A is:\n\nsqrt( [( (a cos \u03b1 + 1)/2 - 0 )^2 + ( (a sin \u03b1)/2 - 0 )^2 ] ) = sqrt( [ ( (a cos \u03b1 + 1)/2 )^2 + ( (a sin \u03b1)/2 )^2 ] ) = (1/2) sqrt( (a cos \u03b1 + 1)^2 + (a sin \u03b1)^2 )\n\nSimplify:\n\n= (1/2) sqrt( a\u00b2 cos\u00b2 \u03b1 + 2a cos \u03b1 + 1 + a\u00b2 sin\u00b2 \u03b1 )\n\n= (1/2) sqrt( a\u00b2 (cos\u00b2 \u03b1 + sin\u00b2 \u03b1 ) + 2a cos \u03b1 + 1 )\n\n= (1/2) sqrt( a\u00b2 + 2a cos \u03b1 + 1 )\n\nSimilarly, the distance from M to C is the same as to A, since M is the midpoint. Similarly, distance from M to B and D would be half the other diagonal BD.\n\nCompute BD:\n\nBD = sqrt( (a cos \u03b1 - 1)^2 + (a sin \u03b1)^2 )\n\n= sqrt( a\u00b2 cos\u00b2 \u03b1 - 2a cos \u03b1 + 1 + a\u00b2 sin\u00b2 \u03b1 )\n\n= sqrt( a\u00b2 (cos\u00b2 \u03b1 + sin\u00b2 \u03b1 ) - 2a cos \u03b1 + 1 )\n\n= sqrt( a\u00b2 - 2a cos \u03b1 + 1 )\n\nTherefore, the distance from M to B or D is (1/2) sqrt( a\u00b2 - 2a cos \u03b1 + 1 )\n\nSo, the maximum distance from M to any vertex is the maximum of (1/2) sqrt( a\u00b2 + 2a cos \u03b1 + 1 ) and (1/2) sqrt( a\u00b2 - 2a cos \u03b1 + 1 )\n\nBut since a and cos \u03b1 are positive (as \u03b1 is an acute angle, so cos \u03b1 positive, and a is a length, so positive), then sqrt( a\u00b2 + 2a cos \u03b1 + 1 ) > sqrt( a\u00b2 - 2a cos \u03b1 + 1 ), so the maximum distance from M to a vertex is (1/2) sqrt( a\u00b2 + 2a cos \u03b1 + 1 ). Therefore, if this distance is \u22641, then the center is covered by the circle of radius 1 around the farthest vertex. Wait, but which vertex is farthest?\n\nWait, M is equidistant to A and C, and equidistant to B and D. So, the distance from M to A and C is (1/2) sqrt( a\u00b2 + 2a cos \u03b1 + 1 ), and to B and D is (1/2) sqrt( a\u00b2 - 2a cos \u03b1 + 1 ). So, if we require that both of these distances are \u22641, then:\n\n(1/2) sqrt( a\u00b2 + 2a cos \u03b1 + 1 ) \u2264 1\n\nand\n\n(1/2) sqrt( a\u00b2 - 2a cos \u03b1 + 1 ) \u2264 1\n\nBut solving the first inequality:\n\nsqrt( a\u00b2 + 2a cos \u03b1 + 1 ) \u2264 2\n\nSquare both sides:\n\na\u00b2 + 2a cos \u03b1 + 1 \u2264 4\n\na\u00b2 + 2a cos \u03b1 \u2264 3\n\nSimilarly, the second inequality:\n\nsqrt( a\u00b2 - 2a cos \u03b1 + 1 ) \u2264 2\n\nSquare:\n\na\u00b2 - 2a cos \u03b1 + 1 \u2264 4\n\na\u00b2 - 2a cos \u03b1 \u2264 3\n\nBut since a\u00b2 + 2a cos \u03b1 \u2264 3 and a\u00b2 -